+# C-1 — `deposit()` reverts on real USDT. The vault is dead on arrival.
+
+**Severity: Critical (total loss of function).**
+`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:94` — `usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);`
+
+`IERC20.approve` is declared `returns (bool)`. Solidity's high-level call therefore requires at least
+32 bytes of return data. Mainnet USDT is a pre-ERC20-finalisation contract: `approve`, `transfer`
+and `transferFrom` return **nothing**. The decode fails and the whole call frame reverts with empty
+revert data. `SafeERC20.safeTransferFrom` on line 93 handles this correctly; the bare `approve` on
+line 94 does not.
+
+Every deposit reverts. No user can ever get in. This is not conditional on ordering, amount, or
+market state.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/MainnetForkProperties.t.sol'
+Ran 1 test for test/audit/MainnetForkProperties.t.sol:MainnetForkPropertiesTest
+[FAIL: EvmError: Revert] test_Property_DepositSucceedsWithRealMainnetUsdt() (gas: 97913)
+Suite result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 515.16ms (147.21µs CPU time)
+```
+
+The trace shows the exact failure point — USDT's `approve` returns `[Stop]` (zero bytes), and the
+next thing that happens is the revert inside `deposit`:
+
+```
