+# C-3 — The owner can seize an incoming deposit with `setDepositFee`
+
+**Severity: Critical (theft of user funds).**
+`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:126-130` — `setDepositFee` has no upper bound, no timelock, and takes effect
+on the very next block.
+
+Deposit fees are not swept to the owner; they inflate the share price for everyone already holding
+shares. So an owner who holds shares can front-run a pending deposit, raise the fee to 9,999 bps,
+let the deposit land, and lower it again. The depositor mints shares against 0.01% of their money;
+the other 99.99% is credited to the existing shareholders, i.e. the owner.
+
+Measured: owner holding a 1,000 USDT position front-runs a 10,000 USDT deposit. The depositor ends
+up able to redeem **10.99 USDT**. The owner exits with **9,956.04 USDT of profit**.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```
+[FAIL: alice must keep the bulk of her 10,000 USDT: 10989010 <= 9000000000] test_Property_OwnerCannotSeizeAnIncomingDeposit() (gas: 350576)
+```
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/VaultAccounting.t.sol' -vv
+[PASS] test_OwnerCanFrontRunDepositAndSeizeIt() (gas: 299333)
+Logs:
+  alice deposited (6dp): 10000.000000
+  alice can redeem (6dp): 10.989010
+  owner profit on a 1,000 USDT position (6dp): 9956.043957
+```
+
+The same unbounded setter is also a denial-of-service switch — deposits above 100% simply brick the
+entry path:
+
+```
+[PASS] test_SetDepositFeeAboveOneHundredPercentBricksDeposits() (gas: 46602)   # 10_001 bps -> arithmetic underflow on `amount - fee`
+[PASS] test_SetDepositFeeToExactlyOneHundredPercentBricksDeposits() (gas: 47655) # 10_000 bps -> NoSharesMinted
+```
+
+```
+[FAIL: next call did not revert as expected] test_Property_DepositFeeIsBounded() (gas: 36424)
+```
+
+### Fix
+
+Three things, all needed:
+
+1. Cap the fee: `uint256 public constant MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS = 100;` (1%) and
+   `if (newFeeBps > MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS) revert FeeTooHigh();` in both `setDepositFee` and the
+   constructor.
+2. Put `setDepositFee` behind a timelock — announce, wait ≥ 24–48h, then apply — so a fee change can
+   never be sandwiched around a deposit.
+3. Give `deposit` a slippage guard: `deposit(uint256 amount, uint256 minSharesOut)`, reverting if
+   `shares < minSharesOut`. That closes the front-run regardless of governance latency.
+
+Also move ownership to a multisig or timelock contract before deployment.
+
