+# H-2 — `pause()` blocks withdrawals, and ownership can be renounced while paused
+
+**Severity: High (owner can permanently freeze all user funds).**
+`src/UsdtYieldVault.sol:107` — `withdraw` carries `whenNotPaused`.
+
+An emergency pause should stop new risk from entering, not lock existing depositors in. Here a single
+owner transaction stops all exits. `Ownable` also exposes `renounceOwnership()`, so a compromised or
+careless owner can pause and then renounce, after which no address on earth can call `unpause()`.
+Every depositor's balance is frozen permanently. No timelock, no guardian split, no escape hatch.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```
+[FAIL: EnforcedPause()] test_Property_DepositorsCanExitWhilePaused() (gas: 239548)
+```
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/VaultAccounting.t.sol' -vv
+[PASS] test_PauseBlocksWithdrawalsAndTrapsUserFunds() (gas: 207831)
+```
+
+That test pauses, confirms `withdraw` reverts `EnforcedPause()`, calls `renounceOwnership()`,
+confirms `withdraw` still reverts, and asserts `vault.owner() == address(0)` with 10,000 USDT of
+alice's balance still on the books.
+
+### Fix
+
+Drop `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw` — pausing deposits is sufficient to stop the bleeding. If you
+genuinely need to halt exits (e.g. Aave itself is compromised), make it a separate, explicitly named,
+time-boxed switch that auto-expires, rather than the same flag as deposits. Independently, override
+`renounceOwnership()` to revert, and move ownership to a multisig.
+
